%U A396575 9579999,18464908,36075194,69357848,132567002,262039898,499210661,976415792,1913373472,3717786264,7176336716,14184357576
%N A396575 Sum, over all distinct partitions q obtained as the sorted multiplicities of equal parts in a partition of n, of the number of fixed-content necklaces with content q.
%C A396575 For a partition lambda of n, group equal parts of lambda, record the size of each group, and sort these group sizes in weakly decreasing order; denote the resulting partition by r(lambda). Let R(n) = {r(lambda): lambda is a partition of n}; repeated values of r(lambda) are included only once.
%C A396575 For q=(q_1,...,q_k), let M(q) be the number of circular words up to rotation on k colors in which color i occurs q_i times. Then a(n) is the sum of M(q) over the distinct q in R(n).
%C A396575 The operation r forgets the numerical sizes of the parts of lambda but retains how often each distinct size occurs. For example, r((4,4,2,1,1,1))=(3,2,1), since the part sizes 4, 2, and 1 occur 2, 1, and 3 times, respectively.
%C A396575 The analogous sum using fixed-content bracelet counts, that is, dihedral rather than cyclic orbits, is termwise less than or equal to a(n).
%H A396575 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="/A396575/b396575.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..100</a> (terms 1..50 from Andrea Cutri)
%H A396575 Andrea Cutri, <a href="/A396575/b396575_1.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..50</a>
%H A396575 Andrea Cutri, <a href="https://github.com/aku-79/oeis-refined-atlas/blob/main/programs/a396575.py">Python program</a>.
%F A396575 a(n) = Sum_{q in R(n)} M(q), where R(n) = {r(lambda): lambda is a partition of n} and each distinct q is included once. For q=(q_1,...,q_k) with m=Sum_i q_i, M(q) = (1/m) * Sum_{d | gcd(q_1,...,q_k)} phi(d) * (m/d)! / Product_i (q_i/d)!.
%e A396575 R(4) = {(1), (1,1), (2), (2,1), (4)}. Each of these five partitions has one necklace, so a(4)=5.
%e A396575 For n=6 the ten distinct values of r(lambda) are (1), (1,1), (2), (1,1,1), (2,1), (3), (2,2), (3,1), (4,1), and (6). Their necklace counts are respectively 1,1,1,2,1,1,2,1,1,1, giving a(6)=12.
